this year the Yale professor said some  things that scandalized Venetian and  made headlines all over the country  writing in The Wall Street Journal she  told the world that her daughters were  not allowed to get any grade less than  an a they had to be the number one  student in every subject except Jim and  drama she recommended calling children  garbage when they failed to measure up  she said parents of overweight children  should address them as hey fatty she  talked about how when her daughter kept  making mistakes in a piano piece she  called her lazy cowardly self indulgent  and pathetic and threatened to give away  her dollhouse to the Salvation Army she  said her children had to be absolutely  perfect because nothing is fun until  you're good at it overnight professor  amy chua became a celebrity her book  battle hymn of the tiger mother was on  magazine covers and bestseller lists and  chill herself was all over the  television and Internet there are many  reasons why choose but dominated the  media and surged the top of the  bestseller lists some of it may have  been a kind of racial fascination with  this character she created the tough  Chinese tiger mother who demanded  absolute perfection or else some of it  probably had to do with the fact that  books on motherhood touch a nerve some  of it was undoubtedly the fact that she  had a really good publicist but I think  for a lot of people and certainly a lot  of people here at Yale where articles on  to it quickly filled the Yale Daily News  the book struck a chord because a lot of  us have tiger mothers inside us  regardless of what our own mothers were  like many of us have an inner tiger  mother monologue that tells us you have  to get an A and everything you have to  be the best in the class when we can't  measure up to the cultural ideal of  beauty we hear a voice saying hey fatty  you're garbage  when we make a mistake something inside  says you're lazy cowardly self indulgent  and pathetic this inner monologue of  perfectionism does a lot of work for us  we may believe it helped get us to the  Ivy League we may rely on it to carry us  through our future careers but it takes  a toll on us as well when we're no  longer the best in the class we worry  that we aren't ourselves anymore and  when we're trying so hard to be the best  we can forget to eat or sleep or pay  attention to the needs of those around  us sometimes we interpret silence as  criticism sometimes we interpret praise  as criticism sometimes when people love  us unconditionally we have trouble  accepting their love worst of all a lot  of the time we have trouble hearing  anything except that ruthless inner  monologue that relentless voice telling  us be perfect be perfect perfectionism  is a problem and yet here is Jesus on  the Sermon on the mat telling us be  perfect as your heavenly Father is  perfect it's easy for those of us who  are perfectionists to interpret this  commandment as a confirmation of our  inner perfectionist voice as yet another  impossibly high standard that we have to  try to meet but this time instead of  getting an A which is hard enough we  have to be perfect like God which is  impossible we already know we are going  to fail at this and we may worry that  now in addition to that nagging voice in  our heads saying I can't believe you got  a B+ on that midterm I can't believe you  haven't been to the gym this week we're  going to have the voice of Jesus in our  head saying things like I can't believe  you didn't give your coat to that  homeless person on Chapel Street  I can't believe you don't love that mean  girl as much as you do your best friend  but I am here to tell you that Jesus is  not a tiger mother Jesus mail of God's  dazzling perfection but he is not a  perfectionist when Jesus was preaching  perfection on the mountain he was not  trying to reinforce our own  self-involved self-loathing inner  monologues instead he was trying to  interrupt them he was trying to replace  our own imperfect perfectionism with a  new vision God's vision so what is  Jesus's vision of perfection and how is  it different from the perfectionism that  were used to the most important way that  it's different and the most liberating  thing about it is that it's not actually  about us not us by ourselves anyway the  perfection that Jesus describes in this  passage and throughout the Sermon on the  Mount is not about the flawlessness of  isolated individuals this perfection is  not anything that we can attain through  solitary striving or through hours spent  in private prayer or at the library  instead it is always about perfection  expressed in relationship to others  perfection that's only possible in the  midst of a community perfection about  love for others and love for God  beginning with the Beatitudes and  continuing with his commands to be salt  and light Jesus has been preaching a  sermon about how his followers need to  be so transformed by love so full of a  rational mercy an unshakable peace that  they will serve as a kind of flavor  enhancer of the world adding  deliciousness it will become a beacon of  shining warmth and brightness instead of  succeeding perfectly according to the  world standards instead of trying to  perfectly obey the letter of the law  Jesus's followers are to act in ways  that are unintelligible by the world  standards that only make sense in the  light of God in their generosity and  their sense of abundance they will give  freely and their sense of security and  God's love for them they will find  themselves able to love people that it  makes no sense for them to love and as  we learn next week they will be so  oriented to God that they will forget to  worry instead of relying on their inner  monologue and their own standards they  will rely on God for their daily bread  and their daily grades and their daily  sense of purpose and meaning and their  daily motivation but more importantly  they will often forget about themselves  entirely in their curiosity about the  communities around them their love for  others and their desire to help keep  God's kingdom come to earth a lot of us  spend a lot of energy trying to get  Jesus's vision of perfection to fit with  our own self-centered vision that energy  is wasted energy a lot of us spend a lot  of time trying to perfectly measure up  to Yale standards while trying to follow  God as well that time is wasted time you  cannot serve both God and Yale our  purpose as members of the Yale community  is not to orient ourselves to the stark  standards of grades and academic honors  and the more nebulous but still harsh  standards of social acceptance instead  our mission is to pursue a different  perfection entirely a friend of mine who  teaches Renaissance literature here told  me that in the 1600s the word perfect  meant full or complete we are perfect  when our hearts are full of love when we  are participating in life with our whole  soul and with all our mind and strength  the wonderful thing about this kind of  perfection is that a teaspoon can be as  completely full as  ocean floor God may be an ocean and we  may be just a teaspoon but we can still  partake in God's perfection by being  full to the brim with love in some ways  I have a special perspective on Yale  perfectionism because right now and both  a student and a teacher I'm taking  classes at the divinity school but I'm  also a TF for English and American  Studies classes in Yale College as a  student I sometimes struggle to silence  my inner monologue when I write I often  have to remind myself that Jesus is  perfection as the kind that counts but  as a teacher I know exactly what a  perfect section is and it's not one  where everyone gets A's neither is it a  section where I have all the answers and  always make amazing lesson plans that  turn out exactly the way I planned no  the perfect section is one where the  students and I are fully present not  just physically present completely  present they may not always like the  reading but they care about it they  contribute to the conversation even if  they're not quite sure they're right  they come with questions and they leave  with questions they listen to each other  with attentiveness and respect when they  meet with me to talk about their papers  it's obvious they're thinking through  ideas that really matter to them the  class is giving them a window into  worlds they haven't known before to  Kenzie and England Harlem in the 1920s  Vietnam in the 1970s and this is helping  them understand their own world in new  ways they may be freshmen who have never  written a college paper before or they  may be seniors who seem to have  everything figured out but either way  they learn and grow over the course of  the semester and through participating  in conversations with them and reading  their work I am learning and growing too  and on the last day of class even though  we're all tired and antsy and ready for  vacation we have a sense  shared satisfaction new friendships have  been formed new favorite authors have  been discovered new English or history  majors have been born and as we say our  goodbyes and maybe talk a little bit  about what's going to be on the final we  feel like we've been traveling together  and we've made progress together on our  journeys we've helped each other along  the way this is the perfection that  Jesus is talking about a collective  expression of wholeheartedness fullness  and completeness that may involve some  mistakes along the way there's much more  I could say about this but for now I  want to close with a part of the gospel  it would be easy to read right by the  part that says God makes the Sun Rise on  the evil and on the good and sends rain  on the righteous and on the unrighteous  on Friday when the temperature  skyrocketed into the 50s and the snow  finally started to melt a little bit you  could walk down the street and see  smiling people people who had taken off  their hats and scarves for the first  time in months people with hope in their  eyes the sunshine and warmth belonged to  everyone sometimes when we're fighting  to attain worldly perfection and a Place  in the Sun it can seem like there's a  limited amount of sunshine in the world  and not everyone gets it and we have to  earn it ourselves when we're in the grip  of perfectionism we can forget that God  makes the Sun Shine on all of us  regardless of whether we deserve it or  whether we feel like we deserve it our  perfect God warms us all with the same  Sun and washes us all with the same rain  some theologians call this common grace  it's the grace that God extends to all  of us all the time regardless of what we  do or what we don't do simply because we  exist amen  you
 

